## ⚙️ Non-Negotiable Agent Rules

```
RULE 1 — NO HARDCODING
  Every attribute, value, finding, and metric must be derived from
  actual files in the repository. Never assume a governance structure,
  compliance control, or risk pattern not evidenced by code or config.

RULE 2 — CITE EVERY FINDING
  Every table row must reference the specific file, annotation, config key,
  class name, or pipeline step that evidences it.
  Format: [FileName] → [specific signal]
  Format for absence: "Not detected — [files scanned]"

RULE 3 — THREE-STATE EVERY CONTROL
  Every governance control must be assessed as one of:
    ✅ ENFORCED    — evidence of active enforcement found
    ⚠️ IMPLIED     — partial or indirect evidence only
    ⛔ NOT DETECTED — scanned relevant files; signal absent
  Never leave a control unrated.

RULE 4 — TOGAF DOMAIN MATURITY SCALE
  Rate each TOGAF domain on a 5-level scale:
    1 — Initial    (ad-hoc, undocumented)
    2 — Managed    (some process, inconsistently applied)
    3 — Defined    (documented, consistently applied)
    4 — Measured   (quantitative metrics tracked)
    5 — Optimizing (continuous improvement in place)
  Derive from evidence only. Never assign a level without justification.

RULE 5 — RISK LEVEL ASSIGNMENT
  CRITICAL — Immediate production or compliance risk
  HIGH     — Reliability or security risk, no mitigation found
  MEDIUM   — Quality or governance risk with partial mitigation
  LOW      — Minor improvement opportunity

RULE 6 — NEVER EXPOSE SECRETS
  Flag credential patterns by file name and type only. Never print values.

RULE 7 — PRESERVE EXACT NAMES
  Use exact class names, annotation values, config keys, endpoint paths,
  pipeline step names, enum values as found in source.

RULE 8 — DUAL-REPO MODE
  When two repositories are provided, analyze each independently,
  then produce side-by-side comparison columns in all tables.
  Label all repo-specific findings [REPO-1] or [REPO-2].

RULE 9 — QUALITY GATE BEFORE OUTPUT
  Run the checklist in Part 10 before writing the final file.
```

